IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `era-types`
IMPORT `era-periods`

-- Employment Rights Act 1996 (UK) — GOAL 1: MINIMUM NOTICE (s 86), and the
-- later effective date of termination it gives (ss 97(2)-(5), 145(5)-(6)).
--
--   "How many weeks' notice must the employer give, and the employee; and
--    when would the employer's statutory notice expire?"
--
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1996-18.txt — ss 86, 97, 145.

§ `Goal 1 — minimum notice`

-- s 86(1) "The notice required to be given by an employer to terminate the
-- contract of employment of a person who has been continuously employed for
-- one month or more— (a) is not less than one week's notice if his period of
-- continuous employment is less than two years, (b) is not less than one
-- week's notice for each year of continuous employment if his period of
-- continuous employment is two years or more but less than twelve years, and
-- (c) is not less than twelve weeks' notice if his period of continuous
-- employment is twelve years or more." The length is ascertained on the day
-- the notice is given (s 211(1)(b)). 0: s 86 requires none.
@ref s 86(1)
@export The minimum notice, in weeks, an employer must give under s 86(1) of the Employment Rights Act 1996 (UK) to end the contract of an employee continuously employed up to a given day: none under one month, one week under two years, one week for each whole year from two to twelve years, and twelve weeks from twelve years. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN emp IS AN `Employee`  @desc The day continuous employment began (date of birth and week's pay are not used)
      d   IS A DATE         @desc The day notice is given
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the notice in weeks the employer must give` emp `on` d MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF   NOT (emp `has been continuously employed for` 1 `months ending with` d) THEN 0
        IF   NOT (emp `has been continuously employed for` 24 `months ending with` d) THEN 1
        OTHERWISE `the lesser of` (`the whole years of continuous employment of` emp `ending with` d) 12

-- s 86(2) "The notice required to be given by an employee who has been
-- continuously employed for one month or more to terminate his contract of
-- employment is not less than one week."
@ref s 86(2)
@export The minimum notice, in weeks, an employee must give under s 86(2) of the Employment Rights Act 1996 (UK): one week once continuously employed for one month, otherwise none. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN emp IS AN `Employee`  @desc The day continuous employment began
      d   IS A DATE         @desc The day notice is given
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the notice in weeks the employee must give` emp `on` d MEANS
    IF   emp `has been continuously employed for` 1 `months ending with` d
    THEN 1
    ELSE 0

-- Notice of w weeks given on day d runs from the next day, so its last day is
-- d + 7w.
@ref s 86(1)
@export The earliest day on which the employer's statutory minimum notice under s 86(1) of the Employment Rights Act 1996 (UK), given on a day, can expire: that day plus seven days for each week of notice required. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN d   IS A DATE         @desc The day notice is given
      emp IS AN `Employee`  @desc The day continuous employment began
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the employer's statutory notice given on` d `to` emp `would expire` MEANS
    `the day` (7 TIMES `the notice in weeks the employer must give` emp `on` d) `days after` d

§§ `The material date and the later date (ss 97(2)-(5), 145(5)-(6))`

-- ss 97(3), (5), 145(6): "the material date" means the date notice was
-- given, or, where none was given, the date the contract was terminated.
@ref ss 97(3), 97(5), 145(6)
GIVEN t IS A `Termination`
GIVETH A DATE
`the material date of` t MEANS
    CONSIDER t's `notice given on`
    WHEN JUST d  THEN d
    WHEN NOTHING THEN t's `ended on`

-- s 97(1): "the effective date of termination" is the day the notice
-- expires, or the termination takes effect.
@ref s 97(1)
GIVEN t IS A `Termination`
GIVETH A DATE
`the effective date of termination under s 97(1) of` t MEANS t's `ended on`

-- s 97(2) "Where— (a) the contract of employment is terminated by the
-- employer, and (b) the notice required by section 86 to be given by an
-- employer would, if duly given on the material date, expire on a date later
-- than the effective date of termination ... for the purposes of sections
-- 108(1), 119(1) and 227(3) the later date is the effective date of
-- termination." s 97(4) does the same where "(a) the contract of employment
-- is terminated by the employee, (b) the material date does not fall during a
-- period of notice given by the employer".
@ref s 97(2), (4)
@export The effective date of termination for ss 108(1), 119(1) and 227(3) of the Employment Rights Act 1996 (UK): the day the notice expired or the termination took effect, or, where the employer terminated (or the employee did, outside a period of the employer's notice), the later day on which the employer's s 86 notice would have expired if given on the material date. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN emp IS AN `Employee`     @desc The day continuous employment began
      t   IS A `Termination`   @desc Who ended the contract, the day notice was given, the day it ended, and whether the employer's notice was running
GIVETH A DATE
`the effective date of termination for ss 108(1) and 119(1) of` emp `and` t MEANS
    IF   extends
    THEN `the later of` (t's `ended on`) (`the day the employer's statutory notice given on` (`the material date of` t) `to` emp `would expire`)
    ELSE t's `ended on`
    WHERE
        extends MEANS
            CONSIDER t's `how it ended`
            WHEN `terminated by the employer` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `terminated by the employee, entitled to do so without notice by the employer's conduct` THEN NOT (t's `during the employer's notice`)
            WHEN `terminated by the employee otherwise` THEN NOT (t's `during the employer's notice`)
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 145(2): the relevant date is the day the notice expires, or the
-- termination takes effect. s 145(5) "Where— (a) the contract of employment
-- is terminated by the employer, and (b) the notice required by section 86 to
-- be given by an employer would, if duly given on the material date, expire
-- on a date later than the relevant date ... for the purposes of sections
-- 155, 162(1) and 227(3) the later date is the relevant date." Unlike
-- s 97(4), there is no limb for the employee terminating.
@ref s 145(2)
GIVEN t IS A `Termination`
GIVETH A DATE
`the relevant date under s 145(2) of` t MEANS t's `ended on`

@ref s 145(5)
@export The relevant date for ss 155, 162(1) and 227(3) of the Employment Rights Act 1996 (UK): the day the notice expired or the termination took effect, or, where the employer terminated the contract, the later day on which the employer's s 86 notice would have expired if given on the material date. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN emp IS AN `Employee`     @desc The day continuous employment began
      t   IS A `Termination`   @desc Who ended the contract, the day notice was given and the day it ended
GIVETH A DATE
`the relevant date for ss 155 and 162(1) of` emp `and` t MEANS
    CONSIDER t's `how it ended`
    WHEN `terminated by the employer` THEN
        `the later of` (t's `ended on`) (`the day the employer's statutory notice given on` (`the material date of` t) `to` emp `would expire`)
    OTHERWISE t's `ended on`
